# AWS training

This guide applies to the end_to_end tier.

## Scope

The template provides:

- An isolated training entry point using the project's training adapters.
- Local SQLite-backed MLflow tracking for each job.
- A portable model candidate with serving artifacts and checksums.
- A non-root training container.
- A Fargate adapter for S3 input download and candidate upload.
- An optional Fargate task definition and scoped IAM roles.
- A SageMaker training-request generator and execution role.

Generated projects still require a project-specific implementation of
`build_project_training_pipeline`. The generated placeholder cannot train
a business model.

The job does not register a model, assign registry aliases, activate a
serving release, or deploy the API.

## Verification status

Automated checks cover:

- Directory isolation and protection of existing results.
- Real local MLflow training, candidate export and model reload.
- Serving-artifact and model-file checksums.
- S3 transport using a simulated client.
- Training inside a network-disabled container with synthetic mounts.
- Container image vulnerability scans.
- SageMaker request generation.
- Terraform initialization and validation.

Cloud execution of the generated Fargate and SageMaker configurations
has not been verified.

The container mount test uses explicitly writable synthetic output
directories. It does not prove that SageMaker's mounted directories
have compatible permissions for UID 10001.

## Implement the project pipeline

Implement the factory in:

`src/{{ package_name }}/pipeline/project_factory.py`

Provide the existing ingestion, feature-building, splitting, training,
evaluation, model-logging and serving-input adapters.

The trainer must use `get_active_training_run_id()` for its TrainingResult.
The job starts the MLflow run before invoking the trainer.

The job overrides these paths:

| Config key | Job location |
| --- | --- |
| data_root | work/data |
| raw | input directory |
| interim | work/data/interim |
| processed | work/data/processed |
| artifacts | work/artifacts |
| models | work/models |

Additional named paths require an explicit isolation mapping in
`aws/train.py`. Unknown keys are rejected.

Serving artifact sources must be local files inside the job input or work
directory. Their destination paths must be unique relative POSIX paths.

Install all dependencies required by the project's model and adapters
before building the training image. No packages are installed during training.

## Run locally

```bash
uv sync --extra aws

uv run --extra aws python -m {{ package_name }}.aws.train \
  --config configs/dev.yaml \
  --input-dir data/raw \
  --work-dir artifacts/training-check/work \
  --model-dir artifacts/training-check/model \
  --output-dir artifacts/training-check/output
```

Input must exist. Work, model and output directories must be empty or absent.
All four directories must be separate and must not contain each other.

The YAML configuration is loaded directly. The application's cloud path
override is not applied. Tracking and registry settings are temporarily
redirected to the job's local backend.

This isolates the framework's configured paths and tracking state; it is
not a security sandbox for arbitrary project-specific Python code.

## Candidate layout

```text
model/
  model/
    MLmodel
    ...
  serving_artifacts/
    ...
  candidate.json
output/
  evaluation.json
```

`candidate.json` records the run ID, task type, model type, project metadata,
artifact references, model-file checksums and `promoted: false`.

`evaluation.json` records metrics, evaluation approval and rejection reasons.

Rejected candidates are also exported for inspection. Successful job
execution does not imply evaluation approval or promotion.

A candidate must be reviewed and converted into a serving release through
the project's release workflow before it is used by the API.
Uploading a candidate alone does not change the active API model.

## Build and test the container

```bash
uv sync --extra aws

docker build \
  -f Dockerfile.training \
  -t {{ project_slug }}-training:local \
  .

docker run --rm {{ project_slug }}-training:local

uv run --extra aws python scripts/training_container_smoke_test.py \
  --image {{ project_slug }}-training:local
```

The default command prints CLI help.

The smoke test uses synthetic adapters from the integration test. It does
not execute the project's production training implementation.

## Prepare AWS resources

Follow the base infrastructure setup in [AWS deployment](aws-deployment.md).
Keep API serving disabled unless an API deployment is also needed.

The end-to-end tier adds a training ECR repository, roles, log group and
security group.

Build and push the training image to the repository identified by the
`training_repository_url` Terraform output. Use the API guide's ECR login
and image-push procedure, replacing the Dockerfile with Dockerfile.training
and selecting the training repository.

Use a Linux/amd64 image and pin its image manifest digest.

Set the local Terraform variables:

```hcl
training_task_enabled = true
training_image_digest = "sha256:REPLACE_WITH_ACTUAL_DIGEST"
```

Review and apply a Terraform plan to register the task definition.
Applying Terraform does not start a training task.

Upload the project's required input files under the `training_input_uri`
prefix. Preserve the relative file layout expected by the ingestor.

## Fargate execution

Run the task explicitly with AWS ECS RunTask.

Use these Terraform outputs:

- training_cluster_arn
- training_task_definition_arn
- training_subnet_ids
- training_security_group_id

Select FARGATE launch type, Linux platform version 1.4.0 and
assignPublicIp=ENABLED.

The task defaults to:

- Input: training_input_uri
- Output: training_output_uri
- Config: configs/dev.yaml
- Training subprocess timeout: 900 seconds

For versioned input prefixes or a different config, override the training
container's command when submitting the task.

Download and upload time are outside the subprocess timeout. Stop the ECS
task explicitly if the overall job runs longer than intended.

Each execution writes to a unique `fargate-...` result prefix. The adapter
uploads model and evaluation files, then writes completion.json last.

Treat a result prefix without completion.json as incomplete. Failed uploads
can leave partial results requiring cleanup.

Verify all of the following:

1. ECS task stopped with container exit code 0.
2. completion.json has status completed.
3. Candidate and evaluation run IDs agree.
4. Evaluation approval is reviewed separately.
5. Downloaded artifacts match their checksums.
6. The exported model reloads and produces expected predictions.

The S3 adapter's completion marker includes an inventory of uploaded files
and their SHA-256 checksums.

## SageMaker execution

Generate a request without submitting it:

```bash
uv run --extra aws python -m {{ package_name }}.aws.sagemaker_config \
  --job-name your-unique-training-job \
  --role-arn YOUR_SAGEMAKER_TRAINING_ROLE_ARN \
  --image-uri YOUR_TRAINING_REPOSITORY_URI@sha256:YOUR_IMAGE_DIGEST \
  --input-uri s3://YOUR_BUCKET/training/input \
  --output-uri s3://YOUR_BUCKET/training/output \
  --kms-key-arn YOUR_STORAGE_KMS_KEY_ARN \
  > training-job.json
```

Use the Terraform outputs sagemaker_training_role_arn,
training_repository_url and storage_kms_key_arn.

Review the generated request before submitting it:

```bash
aws sagemaker create-training-job \
  --cli-input-json file://training-job.json
```

The generated request uses one ml.m5.xlarge instance, a 20 GiB encrypted
volume, a 900-second runtime limit and network isolation.

The selected region must have sufficient training-instance quota.
The submitting identity also needs the relevant SageMaker, iam:PassRole,
and KMS permissions. The service role does not grant these to the operator.

SageMaker stages input files and collects model/output files. The container
runs the common local training entry point, not the Fargate S3 adapter.

Use DescribeTrainingJob to inspect status, failure reason and the
ModelArtifacts.S3ModelArtifacts location. SageMaker packages artifacts;
its S3 layout differs from the Fargate adapter's result prefix.
It does not create the Fargate completion.json marker.

Non-root access to SageMaker-mounted directories remains unverified.
Do not assume a successful local mount test guarantees cloud execution.

## Logs and cleanup

Fargate logs use the Terraform-managed training_log_group.

SageMaker uses the shared /aws/sagemaker/TrainingJobs log group.
This shared group is not managed by this project's Terraform configuration.
Review retention and remove only this project's log streams when appropriate.

Stop active training jobs before destroying infrastructure.

For full teardown, follow [AWS deployment](aws-deployment.md), and also
handle the training ECR repository:

- Temporarily set force_delete = true on aws_ecr_repository.training.
- Apply the deletion-option changes before creating the destroy plan.
- Verify both image repositories and all S3 object versions are included
  in the intended permanent cleanup.
- Reset temporary deletion options in the source afterward.

A Terraform destroy does not remove SageMaker job history or unmanaged
shared logs. Review those separately.

Training inputs, candidate outputs, images, logs, KMS keys and Secrets
Manager resources can incur costs even while no training task is running.